trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Polysomnography confirms two critical findings: (1) normal sleep efficiency — ruling out significant sleep fragmentation from apnea or movements; and (2) elevated melatonin in the early evening, which is objective biochemical evidence of an endogenously advanced circadian phase — the hallmark of Advanced Sleep Phase Disorder (ASPD). This, combined with the fixed 6 p.m. sleep onset, absence of neurological/psychiatric comorbidities, no sedating medications, and stable actigraphy pattern, makes ASPD the overwhelmingly most likely diagnosis. All eight evidence domains have now been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — early sleep since retirement;  
2) Course — consistent, daily, fixed timing;  
3) Associated symptoms — none reported;  
4) PMHx — no neurological or sleep disorders;  
5) Medications — none sedating;  
6) Social/exposure — though not quantified, no contradictory behaviors (e.g., late-night light);  
7) Physical exam screen — normal vision;  
8) Testing — actigraphy + PSG with melatonin elevation confirming endogenous phase advance.  
No further information is needed to reach diagnostic certainty.

[FINISH]

